WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.760
We ask a lot of questions
about our diet.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
What’s the right way to
treat a chronic illness?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.720
Fight off a virus,
lose weight?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.640
The problem is, we get
a lot of different answers.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.880
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.720
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.600
Did you know that if you
buy vitamin E supplements

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.520
you may in fact be paying
to live a shorter life?

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.000
Here’s our first story.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.920
Randomized controlled trials

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.400
show that daily
vitamin D supplementation

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.640
appears to reduce the risk
of acute respiratory infections

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.560
in children and adolescents,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.080
but it does not seem
to make a difference in adults,

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.440
nor does D appear effective

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.000
for boosting
antibody responses

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.360
to influenza vaccination.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.720
What about vitamin E?

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.920
Most places, you'll read

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.240
that there are eight different
tocopherols and tocotrienols

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.400
that are collectively known
as vitamin E,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.760
but only alpha-tocopherol
has been shown

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.040
to protect against
human vitamin E deficiency.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.480
So alpha-tocopherol
is the only bona fide vitamin E

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.440
and is the one found
in vitamin E supplements.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.440
There are observational data
suggesting

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.240
those self-reporting
supplementation with vitamin E

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.040
have, for example,
better pneumonia outcomes,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.040
but only interventional trials
can prove if this is the case.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.360
Compared to placebo supplements,

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.440
vitamin E was able
to significantly boost immunity

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.280
to hepatitis B
and tetanus vaccinations

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.560
(though not to diphtheria
or pneumonia).

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.000
One randomized controlled trial

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.640
found that vitamin E
supplementation of the elderly

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.560
did not cut down on lower
respiratory tract infections

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.080
(like pneumonia)
but did reduce the incidence

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.040
of upper respiratory tract
infections

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.360
(like the common cold)

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.560
by about 20 percent
over one year.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.840
But another study
using the same dose

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.000
found no preventive benefit,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.880
and vitamin E supplementation
actually made the infections

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.280
they did get even worse,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.000
with a greater number
and severity of symptoms,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.080
including a greater likelihood
of fever

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.040
and activity restriction.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.200
By far the largest study
on vitamin E

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.640
and respiratory infections

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.400
was the ATBC Study
that randomized

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.160
nearly 15,000 smokers
to vitamin E or placebo

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.080
for about 5 years.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.480
No overall effect
was found either way

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.720
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.920
but upon subgroup analysis

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.840
it appeared
that while lighter smokers

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.720
living in cities
appeared to benefit

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.280
with about a 50 percent
lower risk

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.880
of catching the common cold,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.040
vitamin E appeared
to be a detriment

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.400
to heavier smokers
living outside of cities,

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.880
resulting in about a 50 percent
increased risk.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.000
"Accordingly,"
concluded the investigators,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.440
"caution should be maintained

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.760
in public health
recommendations

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.120
on vitamin E supplementation

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.480
until its effects
are better understood."

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.640
The pneumonia results
from the same study

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.240
took a similar turn.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.520
No overall effect of vitamin E
compared to placebo,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.080
but upon further analysis,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.080
vitamin E appeared
to decrease pneumonia risk

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.320
among lighter smokers
who exercised,

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.080
yet increased pneumonia risk

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.560
among heavier smokers
who did not.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.280
Higher pneumonia rates
were also noted

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.960
in those randomized
to vitamin D

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.960
and were either low
or high body weight,

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.840
but only, apparently,
among those

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.640
with higher dietary
vitamin C intake.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.240
Heavy smokers
who got a lot of C

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.120
also seemed to have
higher tuberculosis rates on E

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.080
compared to placebo.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.200
This kind of slicing
and dicing of data

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.400
is frowned upon, as it can
spawn spurious connections,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.480
so these findings are regarded
only as exploratory

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.720
for further
research directions.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.360
Overall mortality
in the ATBC Study was similar

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.800
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.400
though again
there were subgroups

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.760
that appeared to have
their lives either cut short

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.280
or extended.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.960
There have been dozens
of controlled clinical trials

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:49.960
on vitamin E,

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.240
and most meta-analyses
found a small

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.640
but apparent real increase
in the risk of mortality

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.880
among those randomized
to vitamin E.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.160
In other words, those who buy
vitamin E supplements

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.240
may be, in effect, paying
to live a shorter life.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.840
Across 46 trials,
12 percent died

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.400
in the vitamin E groups

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.920
versus about 10 percent
in the placebo groups.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.800
This is similar to the increase
in mortality

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.040
seen with beta carotene
supplementation-

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.680
approximately 14 percent
versus 11 percent

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.800
across 26 randomized trials.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.320
The goal of the ATBC

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.560
was to see if taking
antioxidant supplements

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.680
like vitamin E or beta carotene

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.560
could help prevent lung cancer
in smokers.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.000
Vitamin E had no effect
on lung cancer,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.960
and beta-carotene unexpectedly
actually increased

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.520
lung cancer rates,

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.560
but vitamin E did,
incidentally,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.120
appear to reduce
incident prostate cancer rates.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.960
This helped inspire
the SELECT study,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.200
the largest cancer
prevention trial ever performed.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.440
More than 35,000 men were
randomized to take vitamin E,

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.720
selenium, both,
or neither (just placebos).

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.680
The selenium
didn't appear to matter,

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.160
but those randomized
to the straight vitamin E group

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.880
ended up with a 17 percent
greater risk

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.920
of developing prostate cancer.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.720
If 100 men took vitamin E
for a decade,

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.360
we would expect to see
one or two of them

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.160
to be diagnosed
with prostate cancer

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.200
that they otherwise
wouldn't have gotten

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:24.720
had they wasted their money
on sugar pills instead.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.880
Can vitamin E or selenium supplements
prevent or treat Alzheimer's?

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.319
Here are some answers.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.360
Oxidative stress
from free radicals

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.760
is the one of the pathways

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.880
implicated in the development
and deterioration

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.720
of Alzheimer's brains,

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.080
given the evidence of excess
oxidative DNA damage on autopsy.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.480
So, what about trying
an antioxidant?

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.200
Vitamin E levels
tend to be lower

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.440
in the bloodstreams of those
with cognitive impairment

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.920
or dementia,
but which came first?

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.040
Maybe Alzheimer's disease
led to poor eating,

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.080
rather than poor eating

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.640
leading to Alzheimer's disease.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.440
Greater vitamin E intake
from foods

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.440
is clearly associated
with a lower risk

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.680
of developing Alzheimer's,

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.280
but greater vitamin E intake
from supplements is not.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.800
But, that didn't stop
researchers

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.600
from including a vitamin E arm

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.480
in the largest primary
prevention study

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.240
of Alzheimer's disease to date.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.040
The Prevention
of Alzheimer's Disease

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.520
by Vitamin E and Selenium trial

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.720
randomized more than
7500 older men

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.240
to take vitamin E, selenium,
both, or neither-just placebos-

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.840
for five years.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.800
To quote from the conclusion:

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.480
"Neither supplement
prevented dementia."

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.640
A separate long-term vitamin E
supplementation trial

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.600
of older women similarly found
no protection

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.520
against cognitive decline.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.800
Neither did vitamin E
appear to benefit those

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.840
with mild cognitive impairment,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.080
but the trials on Alzheimer's
patients themselves

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.320
started to get more interesting.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:55.480
The first trial,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.200
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine,

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.080
randomized more than 150
Alzheimer's patients

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.760
to vitamin E or placebo
to see if vitamin E could slow

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.920
the progression of the disease.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.040
The primary outcome
was the number of days

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.320
until of one of any
of the following happened:

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.920
death, institutionalization,

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:15.320
loss of the ability
to perform basic activities

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:16.680
of daily living,

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.760
or sliding into severe dementia,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:20.240
where you suffer
a complete loss

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.440
of sense of time or place.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.120
On first analysis,
vitamin E flopped:

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.760
no significant slowing
of progression.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.760
Digging deeper,
the researchers realized

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.000
that despite
the random allotment,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.320
the placebo group
just by chance

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.640
ended up with patients
with milder disease.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.760
After taking that into account,

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.800
the difference in outcomes

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.560
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.920
widened substantially.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:50.200
Now, this kind of
unplanned post-hoc analysis

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.320
is heavily frowned upon,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.320
but the effect
seemed so sizable

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.120
they went ahead
and published it.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.480
In the placebo group,

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.160
the adjusted time to death
or loss of independence

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.800
was 440 days,
but in the vitamin E group,

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.320
after the baseline cognition
was normalized,

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.720
it was 670 days,
more than seven months later.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.600
However,
given the unorthodox analysis,

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.040
the scientific world waited

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.360
for the study to be replicated.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.080
And here we go:
more than 600 patients

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.000
with mild to moderate
Alzheimer's disease

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.640
were subsequently randomized
to vitamin E supplements,

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.800
the drug memantine, both,
or neither (just placebos)

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.400
for two years.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.600
Although there was no slowing
of cognitive decline

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.920
after adjusting
for multiple comparisons,

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:37.480
there was a slowing
of functional decline,

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.560
in terms of managing
daily activities

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.560
like bathing or dressing.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.720
The vitamin E group
appeared to only suffer

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.880
about 1.5 years
of functional deterioration

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.280
in the two years' time,

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.240
resulting in approximately
two hours less caregiver time

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.760
required each day
compared to the drug group.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.320
If those were the only two
clinical vitamin E trials,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.120
I would give vitamin E
serious consideration

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.120
for those
with Alzheimer's disease.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.440
But, there's a third,
a six-month trial,

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.360
that suggested vitamin E
may be detrimental

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.880
to cognition
in some Alzheimer's patients.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.355
Obviously, more research
needs to be done.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.800
